IronFlockOS flash kit for NVIDIA Jetson Orin
============================================

This kit writes IronFlockOS onto the NVMe drive of an NVIDIA Jetson Orin
module (Orin Nano, Orin NX, AGX Orin), and the module's QSPI firmware, from a
Linux PC with the module attached over USB in recovery mode.

Not yet proven on hardware: no module has been flashed with this kit or has
started IronFlockOS yet. The kit and the image are tested in CI up to the
flash itself.

The kit holds IronFlockOS's own scripts only (Apache License 2.0, LICENSE).
It does not contain any of NVIDIA's software. While it runs, it downloads
NVIDIA Jetson Linux (the BSP) from NVIDIA and runs NVIDIA's flash tools from
it. NVIDIA licenses the BSP to you under the NVIDIA Driver License
Agreement: the kit shows it and asks you to accept it before it unpacks or
runs anything of NVIDIA's.

The full guide, with what to do when a step fails:
https://github.com/IronFlock/ironflockos/blob/main/docs/targets/jetson-orin.md#flashing-a-module


What you need
-------------

- A PC with an x86-64 CPU and Ubuntu 22.04 or 24.04, not a virtual machine,
  with internet access, sudo, and about 25 GB of free disk space.
- The USB cable from the PC to the carrier board's flashing port (USB-C next
  to the 40-pin header on the AGX Orin Developer Kit; the USB-C port of the
  Orin Nano Developer Kit). Connect it directly, not through a hub.
- An NVMe drive in the carrier board's M.2 slot. Everything on it is erased.
- The device's .flock file from IronFlock, so it enrolls at its first boot.


Flashing
--------

1. Put the module into recovery mode.
   Orin Nano Developer Kit carrier (Orin Nano, Orin NX): unplug the power,
   put a jumper across the REC and GND pins of the button header, plug the
   power in.
   AGX Orin Developer Kit: with it off, hold the Force Recovery button, press
   and release Power, then release Force Recovery.
   `lsusb -d 0955:` on the PC then lists the module.

2. Unpack the kit and run it with the module's board and the .flock:

     tar -xzf ironflockos-jetson-flash-kit-<version>.tar.gz
     cd ironflockos-jetson-flash-kit-<version>
     sudo ./ironflockos-jetson-flash --board jetson-orin-nano-devkit --flock ~/my-device.flock

   Boards: jetson-orin-nano-devkit (Orin Nano and Orin NX on the Orin Nano
   Developer Kit carrier), jetson-orin-nano-devkit-super, jetson-agx-orin-devkit,
   jetson-agx-orin-devkit-super, jetson-agx-orin-devkit-industrial.
   `./ironflockos-jetson-flash --help` lists every option.

3. Read NVIDIA's Agreement and type yes to accept it.

4. The kit downloads the IronFlockOS image of its release and checks it
   against the signed release catalog, downloads NVIDIA's BSP and checks it
   against the SHA-256 the kit pins, installs NVIDIA's host packages, makes
   the flash package and flashes the module (about 15 minutes). Downloads are
   kept in ./ironflockos-jetson/cache for the next run.

5. When the kit says "flashed", the module restarts. On the Orin Nano
   Developer Kit carrier, unplug the power, remove the recovery jumper and
   plug the power in again. The module starts IronFlockOS, enrolls with the
   .flock and appears online in IronFlock.

To flash again, put the module into recovery mode and run the same command.
